│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 24 distinct trainable parameters (one per qubit per rotation      │
│                          │ layer), which is very parameter-heavy for an 8-qubit circuit trained on a small dataset.  │
│                          │ This leads to strong overfitting (train acc 0.88 vs val 0.63, gap 0.31) and poor          │
│                          │ worst-group margin (0.03). Since parameter economy is explicitly rewarded ("fewer         │
│                          │ distinct trainable parameter names ALWAYS scores higher") and the training set is small   │
│                          │ (favoring low-capacity, well-regularized circuits that generalize), I replace the         │
│                          │ per-qubit independent rotation angles with heavily shared parameters across qubits.       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new design keeps the same qualitative structure (single-qubit rotations +             │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor entanglement) but collapses the 24 independent angles down to just 4     │
│                          │ distinct trainable parameters: a shared RY angle, a shared RZ angle, a shared             │
│                          │ parametrized CRZ entangling angle applied along a linear chain, and a second shared RY    │
│                          │ angle after entanglement. Because this ansatz block is repeated multiple times with the   │
│                          │ same parameter values, even this small parameter set can build up rich correlations       │
│                          │ across qubits through repetition, while drastically cutting capacity to reduce            │
│                          │ overfitting and improve robustness/margin on the hardest validation group. The fixed CNOT │
│                          │ entangling layer (no parameters) provides a permutation-symmetric-like coupling structure │
│                          │ that lets the shared single-qubit rotations act differently on each qubit only through    │
│                          │ the different classically-encoded input state on each qubit, preserving expressivity      │
│                          │ without adding parameters.                                                                │
