│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 24 distinct trainable parameters (one RY + two RZ per qubit, all  │
│                          │ independent), which likely overfits on the small training set (train acc 0.88 vs val/test │
│                          │ acc 0.63/0.56 — a large generalization gap) and is penalized heavily for parameter count. │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose a redesign that drastically cuts the parameter count via aggressive             │
│                          │ weight-sharing across qubits (even/odd grouping), while still providing multi-layer       │
│                          │ entanglement (a forward CNOT chain then a backward CNOT chain) to preserve expressivity   │
│                          │ for correlating information across all 8 qubits. This uses only 5 distinct parameters     │
│                          │ total (down from 24), which should:                                                       │
│                          │ 1. Directly boost parameter_efficiency score component.                                   │
│                          │ 2. Reduce the train-test generalization gap by strongly constraining the hypothesis class │
│                          │ (parameter sharing acts as a powerful inductive-bias regularizer, well-suited to very     │
│                          │ small training sets).                                                                     │
│                          │ 3. Preserve entangling structure (bidirectional CNOT chains) so the circuit can still     │
│                          │ build up correlated features across qubits despite fewer free parameters.                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This aligns with the stated scoring priorities: fewer distinct params always scores       │
│                          │ higher, and reducing the train-test gap is rewarded.                                      │
