│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shared_param_reentangled_ansatz                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 11 distinct parameters (one per qubit for the initial RY layer)   │
│                          │ leading to strong overfitting (train 0.94 vs val 0.72, gap 0.24) and a low combined score │
│                          │ despite decent margins. Prior experiments show that fewer distinct parameters correlate   │
│                          │ strongly with higher combined score (the 5-parameter seed scored 0.41 vs the 4-parameter  │
│                          │ seed's 0.39 and the 11-parameter current version's 0.37), confirming that parameter       │
│                          │ economy dominates the score and that per-qubit individualized parameters mainly drive     │
│                          │ overfitting rather than generalizable structure.                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replace the per-qubit RY layer and the two separate RZ layers (rz_pre/rz_post) with a   │
│                          │ compact, heavily shared-parameter design: a global RX layer, a global RY layer, a full    │
│                          │ entangling ring (structural CNOTs, free), a shared-angle CRZ layer along the same ring, a │
│                          │ fixed long-range entangling layer (opposite-qubit CNOTs, free, to boost                   │
│                          │ connectivity/expressivity without adding parameters), a shared RZ layer, and a final      │
│                          │ shared RY layer. This yields only 5 distinct trainable parameters (down from 11) while    │
│                          │ increasing entangling connectivity (full ring + long-range links) to help the model       │
│                          │ separate harder validation groups without needing many trainable rotations, aiming to     │
│                          │ shrink the train/validation gap and increase worst-group margin as seen in the            │
│                          │ better-performing 5-parameter seed.                                                       │
