│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Prior experiments show that increasing circuit depth while keeping the same shared        │
│                          │ parameter names (i.e., reusing the same trainable angles across more entangling layers)   │
│                          │ improves the combined score without any parameter-count penalty (the 5-param, depth-42    │
│                          │ variant outperformed the 4-param, depth-26 variant). Applying this insight directly to    │
│                          │ the current best low-parameter circuit, I duplicate the entire rotation+entangling        │
│                          │ sequence (RY1 -> RZ1 -> CNOT ring -> CRZ ring -> RY2) a second time, reusing the exact    │
│                          │ same parameter names ("ry1", "rz1", "ent1", "ry2"). This doubles the effective depth and  │
│                          │ the number of entangling interactions (giving the optimizer more capacity to build a more │
│                          │ expressive, better-separating feature map) while leaving n_distinct_params unchanged at   │
│                          │ 4, which should improve robustness/generalization (worst-group margin) without any        │
│                          │ parameter-economy cost.                                                                   │
