│ patch_description        │ The current program's full 8-cycle CRZ entangling layer (8 gates sharing "ent1") plus an  │
│                          │ extra long-range CNOT layer adds substantial depth (63) without improving generalization  │
│                          │ — indeed it shows a much larger train-test gap (0.16) than the simpler disjoint-pair      │
│                          │ design (0.03) that scored higher (0.41) in a prior seed. Full-ring parametrized           │
│                          │ entanglement over-couples all qubits with a single shared angle, which likely amplifies   │
│                          │ overfitting on the tiny training set, while the extra long-range CNOT layer only adds     │
│                          │ circuit depth with no trainable benefit.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose reducing entangling depth and coupling strength while keeping the same          │
│                          │ parameter count (still using "ent1"): replace the full 8-edge ring CRZ layer with a       │
│                          │ sparser 4-edge disjoint-pair CRZ layer (matching the better-performing seed's design),    │
│                          │ and drop the redundant long-range CNOT layer. This keeps the parameter-free backbone      │
│                          │ connectivity (from the CNOT ring) for information flow, but reduces the number of         │
│                          │ parametrized entangling gates and total depth, which should shrink the train/validation   │
│                          │ gap and improve worst-group margin without changing the parameter count (still 5 distinct │
│                          │ params).                                                                                  │
