                         Patch Metadata - Gen 30/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ replace_second_cnot_chain_with_shared_crz                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz contains two full linear CNOT chains (7 gates each) sandwiching the    │
│                          │ parametrized rotations, giving high depth (54) and gate count (126) but poor              │
│                          │ generalization (train 0.62 vs test 0.45, gap 0.17). This suggests the second reversed     │
│                          │ CNOT chain adds entangling depth without contributing useful trainable expressivity,      │
│                          │ likely encouraging overfitting to the small training set.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replace the second reversed linear CNOT chain (7 gates, wires 7->0) with a compact      │
│                          │ long-range entangling layer using CRZ gates on the same disjoint qubit pairs used by the  │
│                          │ initial ZZ layer ([0,4],[1,5],[2,6],[3,7]), reusing the existing shared parameter "g"     │
│                          │ instead of introducing a new one. This:                                                   │
│                          │ - Reduces gate count and depth (4 gates instead of 7 in that block, lower depth           │
│                          │ contribution than a full chain),                                                          │
│                          │ - Adds no new trainable parameter (keeps n_distinct_params at 4, satisfying the "fewer    │
│                          │ parameters always better" bias),                                                          │
│                          │ - Reintroduces the same long-range coupling structure as the first entangling layer,      │
│                          │ creating a more symmetric, parameter-efficient circuit that should generalize better      │
│                          │ because the same parameter governs both long-range entangling layers, effectively         │
│                          │ regularizing the entangling structure,                                                    │
│                          │ - Preserves local nearest-neighbor mixing from the first CNOT chain and the               │
│                          │ ry_a/rz_a/ry_c rotations, retaining expressivity where it matters most.                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This targets the stated goals of reducing depth, reducing train/validation gap, and       │
│                          │ keeping parameter count minimal while retaining meaningful entangling capacity.           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
