│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ split_crz_entangler_two_params                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses a single shared CRZ parameter "d" across all four disjoint pairs  │
│                          │ [0,1],[2,3],[4,5],[6,7], which forces identical entangling phase for both "even-indexed"  │
│                          │ and "odd-indexed" pair groups. Splitting this into two shared parameters—d1 for pairs     │
│                          │ [0,1] & [4,5] and d2 for pairs [2,3] & [6,7]—gives the circuit finer-grained control over │
│                          │ correlations between different qubit groups (adjacent-index pairs vs. next-nearest        │
│                          │ pairs), which can help it adapt to more complex/hard-to-separate data patterns without    │
│                          │ adding circuit depth. This costs only one extra distinct parameter (5→6), a modest        │
│                          │ trade-off for potentially much better worst-group margin and validation accuracy,         │
│                          │ consistent with the pattern seen in the second seed program (6 distinct params, 0.42      │
│                          │ score) which outperformed the current single-param version (5 params, 0.41 score, notably │
│                          │ worse train accuracy).                                                                    │
