╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ split_final_ry_parity                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The final global RY(ry_c) layer currently applies the same shared parameter to all 8      │
│                          │ qubits, collapsing the even/odd sub-lattice information created by the preceding          │
│                          │ entangling layers into a single symmetric rotation. Since the best-performing prior       │
│                          │ variant benefited from parity-differentiated rotations (ry_a/ry_b, rz_a/rz_b), we apply   │
│                          │ the same principle to the final readout rotation: split it into ry_c_even (qubits         │
│                          │ 0,2,4,6) and ry_c_odd (qubits 1,3,5,7). This adds only one extra distinct parameter       │
│                          │ (4→5... actually current has 4 distinct, this makes 5) while allowing the classifier to   │
│                          │ set independent decision thresholds for the two parity classes that were entangled        │
│                          │ asymmetrically by the skip-2 CNOT rings (which only connect same-parity qubits). This     │
│                          │ should improve worst-group margin without meaningfully hurting parameter economy or       │
│                          │ generalization.                                                                           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0269                                                                                   │
