                         Patch Metadata - Gen 76/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two highest-margin seeds. From the first seed we keep the parity-split   │
│                          │ initial RY encoding (ry_a on even qubits, ry_b on odd qubits) and the                     │
│                          │ continuously-tunable ZZ-Ising entangling ring, which produced the best worst-group margin │
│                          │ (0.24) of all candidates. From the second seed we keep the idea of a final shared RY      │
│                          │ "unmixing" layer that helped push validation/train accuracy higher. To aggressively cut   │
│                          │ parameter count (which scores higher at every level with no diminishing returns), the     │
│                          │ long-range ZZ coupling is merged into the *same* shared parameter as the nearest-neighbor │
│                          │ ZZ ring (zz_g), and the separate CRZ entangling block from seed 2 is dropped since it     │
│                          │ added a distinct parameter without improving margin. This yields only 5 distinct          │
│                          │ trainable parameters (down from 6 in both seeds), while keeping a rich, fully             │
│                          │ shared-parameter entangling topology (ring + all-to-opposite long range) that lets a      │
│                          │ single scalar coherently control global entanglement strength — a structure that should   │
│                          │ generalize better from few training samples and converge faster due to the reduced        │
│                          │ parameter landscape.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Layout:                                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. RY(ry_a) on qubits {0,2,4,6}, RY(ry_b) on qubits {1,3,5,7} — parity-broken encoding.   │
│                          │ 2. ZZ(zz_g) nearest-neighbor ring over all 8 qubits (0-1,...,7-0) — cheap, tunable        │
│                          │ entanglement.                                                                             │
│                          │ 3. RZ(rz_c) global shared phase mixing on all qubits.                                     │
│                          │ 4. ZZ(zz_g) long-range coupling connecting opposite halves (0-4,1-5,2-6,3-7), reusing the │
│                          │ SAME parameter as step 2 to avoid growing the parameter count while still spreading       │
│                          │ entanglement across the whole register.                                                   │
│                          │ 5. Final RY(ry_d) global shared closing rotation on all qubits.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Total: 5 distinct parameters, moderate depth, fully shared-parameter Ising-ring backbone. │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
